  "description": {
    "ja": {
      "source": "Wikipedia (ja): セイヨウイラクサ",
      "source_url": "https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E3%82%BB%E3%82%A4%E3%83%A8%E3%82%A6%E3%82%A4%E3%83%A9%E3%82%AF%E3%82%B5",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-14",
      "revision": 110444304,
      "text": "セイヨウイラクサ とは、イラクサ科イラクサ属の多年生植物、被子植物である。別名は、ネトルとも呼ばれる。ヨーロッパ原産。"
    },
    "en": {
      "source": "Wikipedia (en): Urtica dioica",
      "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Urtica_dioica",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-14",
      "revision": 1377934389,
      "text": "Urtica dioica, often known as common nettle, burn nettle, stinging nettle, nettle leaf, or just a nettle or stinger, is a herbaceous perennial flowering plant in the family Urticaceae. Originally native to Europe, much of temperate Asia and western North Africa, it is now found worldwide."
    },
    "es": {
      "source": "Wikipedia (es): Urtica dioica",
      "source_url": "https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Urtica_dioica",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-14",
      "revision": 172500666,
      "text": "La ortiga mayor es una hierba cosmopolita de la familia de las Urticaceae originaria de Europa. La característica más conocida de esta planta es la presencia de pelos urticantes cuyo líquido ácido produce una irritación con picor intenso en la piel cuando se la toca o roza."
    },
    "fr": {
      "source": "Wikipedia (fr): Grande ortie",
      "source_url": "https://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grande_ortie",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-14",
      "revision": 231336058,
      "text": "La Grande ortie, encore appelée ortie dioïque ou ortie commune, est une espèce de plantes à fleurs de la famille des Urticaceae et du genre Urtica."
    },
    "pt": {
      "source": "Wikipedia (pt): Urtica dioica",
      "source_url": "https://pt.wikipedia.org/wiki/Urtica_dioica",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-14",
      "revision": 63175895,
      "text": "Urtiga-comum ou Urtica dioica, é uma planta herbácea de floração perene, nativa da Europa, Ásia, norte da África e América do Norte; e é o membro mais conhecido do gênero Urtica. A planta tem pêlos urticantes ocos chamados tricomas em suas folhas e caules, que agem como agulhas hipodérmicas, injetando histamina e outras substâncias químicas que produzem uma sensação de ardor quando contactado por seres humanos e outros animais. A planta tem uma longa história de uso como um medicamento e como uma fonte de alimento."
    },
    "id": {
      "source": "Wikipedia (id): Urtica dioica",
      "source_url": "https://id.wikipedia.org/wiki/Urtica_dioica",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-14",
      "revision": 29892121,
      "text": "Urtica dioica, juga dikenal sebagai jelatang biasa adalah tanaman berbunga abadi herba dalam famili Urticaceae. Awalnya asli ke Eropa, sebagian besar di Asia sedang dan Afrika Utara bagian barat, sekarang ditemukan di seluruh dunia, termasuk Selandia Baru. dan Amerika Utara. Spesies ini dibagi menjadi enam subspesies, lima di antaranya memiliki banyak rambut berongga menyengat yang disebut trikoma pada daun dan batang, yang bertindak seperti jarum hipodermik, menyuntikkan histamin dan bahan kimia lain yang menghasilkan sensasi menyengat pada kontak."
    }
  },
  "sections": {
    "cultivation": {
      "en": {
        "source": "Wikipedia (en): Urtica dioica § Cultivation",
        "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Urtica_dioica#Cultivation",
        "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
        "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
        "retrieved": "2026-10-14",
        "revision": 1379142557,
        "heading": "Cultivation",
        "text": "■ Field\n\n■ Sowing and planting\nThree cultivation techniques can be used for the stinging nettle: 1) direct sowing, 2) growing seedlings in nurseries with subsequent transplantation and 3) vegetative propagation via stolons or head cuttings.\n\nDirect sowing: The seedbed should have a loose and fine structure, but should be reconsolidated using a packer roller imminently prior to sowing. Sowing time can be either in autumn or in spring. Seed density should be 6 kilograms/hectare with row spacing of 30 cm (12 in) and 42–50 cm in autumn and spring, respectively. The disadvantage of direct sowing is that it usually leads to incomplete plant coverage. This drawback can be mitigated by covering the seedbed with a transparent perforated foil in order to improve seed germination. Further, weed control can be problematic as the stinging nettle has a slow seedling development time.\nGrowing seedlings: For this technique pre-germinated seeds are sown between mid-/end-February and beginning of April and grown in nurseries. Seedlings are grown in tuffs with 3–5 plants/tuff and a seed density of 1.2–1.6 kg/1000 tuffs. Faster germination is achieved by alternating high temperature during daytime (30 °C for 8 h) and lower temperature during nighttime (20 °C for 16 h). Before transplanting, the seedlings should be fertilised and acclimated to cold temperatures. Transplantation should start around Mid-April with row spacing of 42–50 cm (17–20 in) and plant spacing within rows of 25–30 cm.\nVegetative propagation: Stolons (with several buds) of 10 cm should be planted from mid-April in a depth of 5–7 cm (2–2+3⁄4 in). Head cuttings are grown in nurseries starting between mid-May and mid-June.",
        "truncated": true
      }
    }
  },
